  "narrative": "All newly delivered conditional comparisons are interpreted using the signed convention. For direction -1 claims, a positive signed estimate means the raw exposed-minus-comparator score is more negative, i.e. stronger CRISPR dependency in the exposed group. For the direction +1 NFE2L2 claims, a positive signed estimate means the raw score is less negative, i.e. weaker dependency in KEAP1-loss cells. The new evidence substantially strengthens the three large prior associations within this dataset. SMARCA4-loss is associated with stronger SMARCA2 dependency not only overall but also in adenocarcinoma, above-median Cas9 activity, and above-median screen ROC cells (H20/H27/H30). MSI-high is associated with stronger WRN dependency overall and in adenocarcinoma, Avana-library cells, above-median Cas9 activity, and above-median screen ROC cells (H21/H22/H26/H29). KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency overall and in the same strict contexts (H25/H28/H31). These conditional signed effects are approximately 0.78 to 0.80 with very tight intervals and cell counts satisfying the 20-per-cell rule. That magnitude is far beyond a 10% relative-scale criterion using the absolute mean dependency scores, and the intervals exclude trivial or modest effects. The consistency across adenocarcinoma, library, and high-quality technical subsets makes these findings less likely to be artifacts of a single screen context, although they still remain dataset-specific associations and not causal claims. I therefore accept the due conditional associations and close the redundant robustness checks. I also close the earlier accepted H8/H17 investigations because the new evidence sufficiently documents context stability for those associations; H5 remains active because I am requesting independent validation for it. H23 shows no support for the hypothesized clinically meaningful stronger SMARCA2 dependency with ARID1A loss: the signed estimate is small and negative, and for the proposed stronger-dependency direction the upper bound corresponds to only about 5.5% of the SMARCA2 mean scale, below the 10% threshold. I reject that directional claim and close it; this does not prove there is no effect, only that the registered hypothesized clinically meaningful association is unsupported. H35 remains unresolved because EGFR-amplified adenocarcinoma cells have a positive signed point estimate of about 0.020, below the 10% scale, but the interval still permits a clinically meaningful stronger EGFR dependency up to roughly 20%. Given the exploratory subgroup nature and the weak/nonreplicated overall EGFR signal, I do not accept it. I propose an interaction test (parent H35) to determine whether the adenocarcinoma EGFR-amplification contrast differs from its complement, which is a cleaner heterogeneity test than treating H35 as an isolated subgroup. The main concern for the large effects is not statistical detection but residual technical confounding, multiplicity from many targeted tests, and generalizability. The large accepted effects are so far above the threshold that multiplicity is not a serious threat, but the smaller EGFR and ARID1A follow-ups are more vulnerable to overinterpretation. I request one independent validation for H5 because MSI-high association with WRN dependency is the largest and most potentially important finding; independent replication will improve confidence in the dataset-level association without implying causality or generalizability.",
