  "narrative": "The strongest evidence so far concerns three large dataset-specific associations: MSI-high with stronger WRN dependency, SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2 dependency, and KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2 dependency. These are already robust across adenocarcinoma and high-quality screen subsets, and WRN/MSI-high has independent validation. Because only 12 analyses can be advanced at a time, I defer currently active but unevaluated redundancy checks (batch, library, low-Cas9, and several small EGFR/ERBB2/RAS follow-ups) to allocate slots to higher-information new comparisons. The new proposals test three useful extensions. First, if MSI-high induces replication/transcription stress, it may not only affect WRN but also DHX9, POLR2A, or USP7; these are falsifiable hypotheses expecting more negative dependency in MSI-high cells. Second, if MYC expression reflects proliferation or transcriptional state, it may modify or at least confirm the accepted large associations within a MYC-high context; I register direct mean differences in MYC-high cells for the three accepted exposure-outcome pairs. Third, I test biologically linked p53/MDM2/USP7/MYC and SWI/SNF-to-transcription hypotheses: MYC amplification may strengthen MDM2 dependency, TP53 loss may weaken USP7 dependency, and SMARCA4 loss may strengthen POLR2A dependency. Finally, I add RAS-pathway cross-dependency tests: BRAF mutation may associate with KRAS dependency, KRAS mutation may associate with BRAF dependency, and KRAS mutation may weaken RIT1 dependency. Signed estimates are interpreted directly: negative signed values support stronger dependency for direction -1 claims, and positive signed values support weaker dependency for direction +1 claims. Given dependency-score means around 0.09–0.42, the 10% clinical threshold corresponds roughly to 0.01–0.04 score units, so many cross-pathway effects may remain clinically non-meaningful unless they are unusually large. All conclusions remain dataset-specific associations, not causal claims or external generalizations.",
  "research_notes": "Current accepted dataset findings: H5 MSI-high stronger WRN dependency (validated), H8 SMARCA4-loss stronger SMARCA2 dependency, H17 KEAP1-loss weaker NFE2L2 dependency, with robustness in adenocarcinoma/high-Cas9/high-ROC/Avana WRN contexts. Negative or unsupported directional claims: H2, original H6, H13, H15, H23. Many small unresolved signals: EGFR, ERBB2, KRAS/BRAF, TP53-MDM2, MYC-USP7/RPL11, KIF18A, STAT3, TMED10. I deferred all active unevaluated claims H18,H24,H32-H46 to prioritize 12 new comparisons: replication/transcription consequences of MSI-high beyond WRN; accepted large effects restricted to MYC-high expression; p53/MDM2/USP7/MYC axis; SMARCA4 loss to POLR2A; and RAS/RTK pathway cross-dependencies. Future plans: run high-priority new tests first; then reactivate direct non-Avana and low-Cas9 robustness if new effects emerge or if accepted-effect technical boundaries remain uncertain. Avoid treating any dataset association as causal or broadly generalizable."
